499
00:26:57,792 --> 00:26:59,585
I'll just call you when I have the information.  

500
00:26:59,669 --> 00:27:01,754
I can't have this phone ringing if I don't expect it.  

501
00:27:01,838 --> 00:27:03,923
That's kind of how phones work.  

502
00:27:04,006 --> 00:27:05,758
If the people around me hear it, there could be trouble.  

503
00:27:05,842 --> 00:27:07,510
<i>What the hell are you involved with?</i>  

504
00:27:09,470 --> 00:27:10,555
Long story.  

505
00:27:10,638 --> 00:27:14,016
Short version, trouble always seems to find you.  

506
00:27:15,059 --> 00:27:16,060
<i>You know,</i>  

507
00:27:16,144 --> 00:27:18,062
my case load's kind of light right now.  

508
00:27:18,146 --> 00:27:20,064
-Why don't I come help? <i>-Not on this one.</i>  

509
00:27:20,148 --> 00:27:23,025
I know the guy running this and I can't have anyone else involved.  

510
00:27:23,109 --> 00:27:25,695
I'm not just anyone else.  

511
00:27:25,778 --> 00:27:27,488
Who are you up against, Reacher?  

512
00:27:27,572 --> 00:27:29,699
[call disconnects]  

513
00:27:46,132 --> 00:27:47,466
All right.  

514
00:27:53,097 --> 00:27:54,682
What the hell's he doing?  

515
00:28:01,522 --> 00:28:02,815
[engine turns off]  

516
00:28:06,986 --> 00:28:09,197
What's with the hold-off?  

517
00:28:09,280 --> 00:28:10,865
In case anyone was following by car,  

518
00:28:10,948 --> 00:28:13,701
I counted 72 seconds from the moment I took this exit.  

519
00:28:13,784 --> 00:28:16,579
That's the amount of time it takes to drive a mile at 50 miles an hour.  

520
00:28:16,662 --> 00:28:19,165
And no one would follow visually at a distance greater than a mile.  

521
00:28:19,248 --> 00:28:20,625
So, no one's tailing you?  

522
00:28:20,708 --> 00:28:22,210
Not physically.  

523
00:28:22,293 --> 00:28:24,754
There doesn't seem to be any kind of tracker hidden in that cab.  

524
00:28:24,837 --> 00:28:28,382
There is this GPS I'm supposed to be following.  

525
00:28:28,466 --> 00:28:30,885
Beck could have someone back at the office watching my route from afar,  

526
00:28:30,968 --> 00:28:33,638
which makes sense considering his operation's light on manpower.  

527
00:28:33,721 --> 00:28:36,182
Yeah, then let's get this done. I've got an old friend coming to help us.  

528
00:28:36,265 --> 00:28:38,309
He should be here any minute.  

529
00:28:43,773 --> 00:28:45,316
It's hers.  

530
00:28:45,399 --> 00:28:47,777
I've seen her in these a hundred times.  

531
00:28:58,579 --> 00:29:00,706
Rugs.  

532
00:29:00,790 --> 00:29:04,252
Better than the alternative, we could've found your girl in there.  

533
00:29:09,215 --> 00:29:11,133
-Hey! -Good to see you, Dickie.  

534
00:29:11,217 --> 00:29:13,219
Can't get enough of that wonderful Duff.  

535
00:29:13,302 --> 00:29:14,595
[chuckles softly]  

536
00:29:14,679 --> 00:29:18,140
Reacher, Villy, this is Dick Grunert.  

537
00:29:18,224 --> 00:29:20,726
Trains drug-sniffing dogs for New Haven PD.  

538
00:29:20,810 --> 00:29:23,354
We worked on a few cases together a couple years back.  

539
00:29:23,437 --> 00:29:25,898
Yeah, uh, and I go by Rich now.  

540
00:29:26,983 --> 00:29:29,568
And this here is Pfizer.  

541
00:29:29,652 --> 00:29:30,945
-Pfizer? -Yeah.  

542
00:29:31,028 --> 00:29:32,697
Because drugs are her business.  

543
00:29:32,780 --> 00:29:34,198
You're a fucking idiot.  

544
00:29:34,282 --> 00:29:36,909
Yeah, but I can train the shit out of a canine.  

545
00:29:36,993 --> 00:29:39,704
Pfizer! <i>Rauschgift!</i>  

546
00:29:39,787 --> 00:29:41,122
There we go. [grunts]  

547
00:29:41,205 --> 00:29:43,332
That's thick plastic wrap on those rugs.  

548
00:29:43,416 --> 00:29:47,503
Well, the dogs I train can smell a Christmas fart on New Year's.  

549
00:29:47,586 --> 00:29:50,006
And Pfizer's the best I've ever worked with.  

550
00:29:50,089 --> 00:29:51,132
Just like Duff.  

551
00:29:51,215 --> 00:29:54,260
Don't know if that's a compliment or an insult.  

552
00:29:54,343 --> 00:29:55,344
[Grunert] Eh.  

553
00:29:55,428 --> 00:29:58,848
Dry as Utah on Sunday. Sorry, Duff. [grunts]  

554
00:29:58,931 --> 00:30:00,391
No drugs.  

555
00:30:04,145 --> 00:30:07,982
If you're trying to prepare yourself for what you might find, don't bother.  

556
00:30:08,065 --> 00:30:11,485
Can't be done. Just ask the question.  

557
00:30:14,822 --> 00:30:17,199
What if there's a cadaver in there?  

558
00:30:17,283 --> 00:30:21,620
Well, if there was a body in there, dead or alive, she would've found it.  

559
00:30:22,663 --> 00:30:24,165
Thanks, Rich.  

560
00:30:25,207 --> 00:30:27,001
Sure thing. Come on, girl.  

561
00:30:28,544 --> 00:30:31,255
No drugs, no body to get rid of.  

562
00:30:31,339 --> 00:30:33,549
What'd they even send you on this trip for?  

563
00:30:33,632 --> 00:30:35,968
[tense music playing]  

564
00:30:36,052 --> 00:30:37,386
It's a loyalty test.  

565
00:30:37,470 --> 00:30:39,597
Russian roulette proves I have guts.  

566
00:30:39,680 --> 00:30:41,932
Making the delivery proves I can be trusted.  

567
00:30:42,016 --> 00:30:44,226
Not if we don't get moving again, right now.  

568
00:30:47,563 --> 00:30:49,148
Hey, we still need to solder the lock.  

569
00:30:49,231 --> 00:30:50,524
I'll handle that.  

570
00:30:50,608 --> 00:30:52,651
Take this GPS and start driving your car.  

571
00:30:52,735 --> 00:30:55,237
If anyone's tracking me, they'll think the truck's en route.  

572
00:30:55,321 --> 00:30:58,741
Pull off at the first rest stop after the first highway sign that reads New London.  

573
00:30:58,824 --> 00:31:00,910
-Duffy and I will meet you there. -Done.  

574
00:31:00,993 --> 00:31:02,244
[sighs]  

575
00:31:02,328 --> 00:31:04,038
You ever used one of those before?  

576
00:31:04,121 --> 00:31:06,165
Just to get a guy to talk.  

577
00:31:06,248 --> 00:31:07,666
[chuckles]  

578
00:31:10,711 --> 00:31:11,754
Oh.  

579
00:31:11,837 --> 00:31:12,963
[♪ The Beta Band: "Dry the Rain"]  

580
00:31:13,047 --> 00:31:17,426
♪ <i>This is the definition of my life</i> ♪  

581
00:31:17,510 --> 00:31:19,804
♪ <i>Lying in bed in the sunlight</i> ♪  

582
00:31:19,887 --> 00:31:22,014
♪ <i>Choking on the vitamin tablet</i> ♪  

583
00:31:22,098 --> 00:31:26,435
-♪ <i>The doctor gave</i> <i>in the hope of saving me...</i> -[phone chimes]  

584
00:31:26,519 --> 00:31:29,563
[scoffs] You're not gonna believe this.  

585
00:31:29,647 --> 00:31:31,107
Try me.  

586
00:31:31,190 --> 00:31:33,776
I had a hunch about something, so I reached out to ICE.  

587
00:31:33,859 --> 00:31:35,820
Ever since we got onto Beck,  

588
00:31:35,903 --> 00:31:39,407
I couldn't square up how he's been able to stay so low profile.  

589
00:31:39,490 --> 00:31:42,743
No flags at Customs, no holdups in shipments.  

590
00:31:42,827 --> 00:31:46,872
So I asked ICE for search-rate stats from ports all over the world.  

591
00:31:46,956 --> 00:31:48,124
That what you got on your phone?  

592
00:31:48,207 --> 00:31:50,501
Yep. And from what I can see,  

593
00:31:50,584 --> 00:31:52,837
Zachary Beck's shipments are searched  

594
00:31:52,920 --> 00:31:55,005
ten times less than anyone else's.  

595
00:31:55,089 --> 00:31:56,799
Always clean?  

596
00:31:56,882 --> 00:31:58,426
As a fucking whistle.  

597
00:31:58,509 --> 00:32:01,095
That search rate reads like he's paying off someone at Customs  

598
00:32:01,178 --> 00:32:02,930
and getting to choose which shipments he wants searched  

599
00:32:03,013 --> 00:32:04,098
just to keep up appearances.  

600
00:32:04,181 --> 00:32:06,183
Mm-hmm. How it seems.  

601
00:32:06,267 --> 00:32:08,018
-[truck horn honks] -Easy, lead foot.  

602
00:32:08,102 --> 00:32:11,105
We need to make up time. We're almost to Villanueva.  

603
00:32:13,816 --> 00:32:16,944
You know, Angel said he, Duke, all of 'em,  

604
00:32:17,027 --> 00:32:20,156
they're not working for Beck. He's not the one giving orders.  

605
00:32:20,239 --> 00:32:21,699
Then who is?  

606
00:32:24,201 --> 00:32:25,828
Quinn?  

607
00:32:25,911 --> 00:32:27,329
Didn't ask.  

608
00:32:27,413 --> 00:32:29,957
Can't let on I know more than I'm supposed to.  

609
00:32:30,040 --> 00:32:32,334
But if we're taking bets,  

610
00:32:32,418 --> 00:32:34,712
I'd say we're getting close.  

611
00:32:34,795 --> 00:32:37,423
♪ <i>Junkyard fool with eyes of gloom...</i>  

612
00:32:37,506 --> 00:32:40,301
Earlier, you told me there was no way to prepare  

613
00:32:40,384 --> 00:32:43,053
for what I might see if Teresa was in the truck.  

614
00:32:44,513 --> 00:32:47,308
You said that like you knew it from experience.  

615
00:32:47,391 --> 00:32:49,101
[somber music playing]  

616
00:32:49,185 --> 00:32:51,896
I worked with a kid like Teresa once.  

617
00:32:51,979 --> 00:32:56,150
She was young, but smart, capable, determined.  

618
00:32:56,233 --> 00:32:58,569
A natural, like you said Teresa was.  

619
00:33:00,571 --> 00:33:01,780
What happened?  

620
00:33:03,282 --> 00:33:05,034
What did Quinn do to her?  

621
00:33:12,666 --> 00:33:13,751
Okay.  

622
00:33:15,461 --> 00:33:16,712
I get it.  

623
00:33:26,555 --> 00:33:28,557
[dramatic music playing]  

624
00:33:33,062 --> 00:33:36,106
[brakes squeal, hiss]  

625
00:33:45,950 --> 00:33:48,494
Well, guess I'll see you soon.  

626
00:33:48,577 --> 00:33:50,037
Guess so.  

627
00:34:03,300 --> 00:34:04,718
[brakes hiss]  

628
00:34:04,802 --> 00:34:06,428
We've been waiting.  

629
00:34:06,512 --> 00:34:07,888
Duke said not to speed.  

630
00:34:07,972 --> 00:34:10,015
He also said don't be late.  

631
00:34:25,030 --> 00:34:26,115
Rugs.  

632
00:34:27,700 --> 00:34:31,078
You work for a rug company. What'd you think was in there?  

633
00:34:32,329 --> 00:34:33,539
Give me the keys.  

634
00:34:36,834 --> 00:34:39,003
Get your ass in that truck, get back to the warehouse.  

635
00:34:39,086 --> 00:34:41,130
[tense music playing]  

636
00:34:45,467 --> 00:34:47,469
♪ ♪  

637
00:35:01,483 --> 00:35:03,360
[brakes squeal, hiss]  

638
00:35:07,406 --> 00:35:08,782
[grunts]  

639
00:35:16,457 --> 00:35:18,459
[suspenseful music playing]  

640
00:35:26,050 --> 00:35:27,593
[Angel] Reacher!  

641
00:35:27,676 --> 00:35:28,844
That you, my man?  

642
00:35:35,601 --> 00:35:37,394
Where is everyone?  

643
00:35:37,478 --> 00:35:41,482
Got off early. Not much to do until the next shipment's in.  

644
00:35:41,565 --> 00:35:44,943
But I had to wait here, for you.  

645
00:35:45,027 --> 00:35:47,363
You were tracking my route.  

646
00:35:49,031 --> 00:35:50,324
Someone had to.  

647
00:35:51,867 --> 00:35:53,410
Where's Duke?  

648
00:35:53,494 --> 00:35:55,663
I thought he was gonna pick me up.  

649
00:35:55,746 --> 00:35:57,706
On the way.  

650
00:35:57,790 --> 00:36:00,376
But I wanted to talk to you first.  

651
00:36:03,962 --> 00:36:04,963
About what?  

652
00:36:06,256 --> 00:36:08,634
Um, there's, um...  

653
00:36:08,717 --> 00:36:12,513
a few things from our trip to the impound lot that  

654
00:36:12,596 --> 00:36:15,099
did not sit right with me, you know?  

655
00:36:16,433 --> 00:36:20,479
At first, I thought you were just stalling in the gas station,  

656
00:36:20,562 --> 00:36:22,481
couldn't figure out why.  

657
00:36:22,564 --> 00:36:26,235
But then, you protected that cop.  

658
00:36:26,318 --> 00:36:28,070
I knocked him out cold.  

659
00:36:28,153 --> 00:36:29,613
Yeah, you didn't let me shoot him.  

660
00:36:30,698 --> 00:36:31,699
Next time, then.  

661
00:36:31,782 --> 00:36:32,783
Eh...  

662
00:36:33,992 --> 00:36:38,122
Then I was dumping the minivan-- you know, the-the one you "hot-wire."  

663
00:36:39,748 --> 00:36:42,209
I decided to take a closer look,  

664
00:36:42,292 --> 00:36:46,088
and there wasn't even a scratch on the steering column.  

665
00:36:47,673 --> 00:36:50,467
You didn't use a screwdriver to break into it.  

666
00:36:50,551 --> 00:36:51,719
I used my hands.  

667
00:36:51,802 --> 00:36:53,387
[sighs] That's impossible.  

668
00:36:53,470 --> 00:36:56,056
I'm strong. Ask Paulie.  

669
00:36:56,140 --> 00:37:01,603
Also, there wasn't any insulation on the mats from stripping the wires either.  

670
00:37:01,687 --> 00:37:03,147
I tossed it.  

671
00:37:03,230 --> 00:37:05,107
[laughs]  

672
00:37:05,190 --> 00:37:07,735
Okay, okay, okay. So,  

673
00:37:07,818 --> 00:37:10,404
while you were in the process of stealing a car,  

674
00:37:10,487 --> 00:37:13,282
you picked up the tiny pieces of insulation  

675
00:37:13,365 --> 00:37:15,534
and threw them out?  

676
00:37:15,617 --> 00:37:16,702
What the fuck for?  

677
00:37:16,785 --> 00:37:19,079
[vehicle approaching]  

678
00:37:19,163 --> 00:37:21,039
[horn honking]  

679
00:37:22,166 --> 00:37:24,752
That's Duke. I need to go.  

680
00:37:25,878 --> 00:37:28,338
There's something I don't like about you, man.  

681
00:37:29,423 --> 00:37:32,301
There's a lot of things I don't like about you, so I guess you're ahead.  

682
00:37:32,384 --> 00:37:33,886
You're dirty.  

683
00:37:35,512 --> 00:37:40,017
I have, like, a seventh sense for these things.  

684
00:37:44,229 --> 00:37:45,439
You're a cop.  

685
00:37:45,522 --> 00:37:47,357
You already knew I was an M.P.  

686
00:37:47,441 --> 00:37:50,861
No, no, no, no, no. I mean you're working with them.  

687
00:37:52,488 --> 00:37:53,906
-[horn honks] -You know what?  

688
00:37:53,989 --> 00:37:56,241
Why don't we ask Duke  

689
00:37:56,325 --> 00:37:58,410
what he thinks of what I just-- [grunts]  

690
00:38:07,377 --> 00:38:08,796
[thunder rumbling]  

691
00:38:11,215 --> 00:38:13,550
[grunting, straining]  

692
00:38:19,765 --> 00:38:21,183
[horn honks]  

693
00:38:21,266 --> 00:38:22,643
[grunting]  

694
00:38:26,605 --> 00:38:28,232
[horn honking]  

695
00:38:31,693 --> 00:38:32,820
[bone cracks]  

696
00:38:32,903 --> 00:38:34,655
[horn honking]  

697
00:38:38,700 --> 00:38:40,244
[honking continues]  

698
00:38:55,133 --> 00:38:58,595
What are you, deaf? What's the holdup?  

699
00:38:58,679 --> 00:39:00,639
Angel talks too much.  

700
00:39:01,723 --> 00:39:03,600
[thunder rumbles]  

701
00:39:03,684 --> 00:39:05,727
[♪ The Defiant: "Dead Language"]  

702
00:39:05,811 --> 00:39:07,813
[singer vocalizing]  

703
00:39:15,237 --> 00:39:19,533
♪ <i>Why go outside and leave this space?</i> ♪  

704
00:39:19,616 --> 00:39:23,161
♪ <i>I've got the A.C. blasting my face</i> ♪  

705
00:39:23,245 --> 00:39:26,456
♪ <i>And I'd feel awkward and out of place </i>♪  

706
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:29,418
♪ <i>I've got everything I need right here</i> ♪  

707
00:39:29,501 --> 00:39:33,130
♪ <i>At my fingertips, literally</i> ♪  

708
00:39:33,213 --> 00:39:37,009
♪ <i>There's an app for everything</i> ♪  

709
00:39:37,092 --> 00:39:40,304
♪ <i>So why go back to reality?</i> ♪  

710
00:39:40,387 --> 00:39:44,182
♪ <i>It's clearly overrated, my dear</i> ♪  

711
00:39:44,266 --> 00:39:49,354
♪ <i>Why fall in love?</i> ♪  

712
00:39:49,438 --> 00:39:52,399
♪ <i>Love is blind</i> ♪  

713
00:39:54,109 --> 00:39:56,778
♪ <i>Plus, who's got that kind of time?</i> ♪  

714
00:39:56,862 --> 00:40:01,074
♪ <i>And now, we walk like zombies</i> <i>in the land of the lost</i> ♪  

715
00:40:01,158 --> 00:40:04,578
♪ <i>A pre-fabricated universe,</i> <i>our eyes are all crossed</i> ♪  

716
00:40:04,661 --> 00:40:08,081
♪ <i>Rest in peace, you dead language</i> <i>and the time that you've cost</i> ♪  

717
00:40:08,165 --> 00:40:10,918
♪ <i>Thank the Lord for opposable thumbs</i> ♪  

718
00:40:11,001 --> 00:40:14,796
♪ <i>Asleep at the wheel,</i> <i>crawling from the curb to the door</i> ♪  

719
00:40:14,880 --> 00:40:18,175
♪ <i>I've had some deep conversations</i> <i>with my living room floor</i> ♪  

720
00:40:18,258 --> 00:40:21,678
♪ <i>I should've died at 27</i> <i>like my heroes before</i> ♪  

721
00:40:21,762 --> 00:40:24,932
♪ <i>But I was too busy having fun</i> ♪  

722
00:40:25,015 --> 00:40:27,851
♪ <i>And now, we're too busy getting dumb</i> ♪  

723
00:40:27,935 --> 00:40:29,937
[singer vocalizing]  

724
00:40:33,065 --> 00:40:35,108
♪ <i>Why fall in love?</i> ♪  

725
00:40:35,192 --> 00:40:36,693
♪ <i>I heard it's blind</i> ♪  

726
00:40:37,778 --> 00:40:40,948
♪ <i>Plus, who's got that kind of time?</i> ♪  

727
00:40:41,031 --> 00:40:44,242
♪ <i>And I can't dance,</i> <i>and I don't drink wine</i> ♪  

728
00:40:44,326 --> 00:40:47,037
♪ <i>So what the hell is in it for me?</i> ♪  

729
00:40:48,080 --> 00:40:52,709
♪ <i>Why fall in love? </i>♪♪ 

